Interferers and room impulse responses for the barge-in measurements under realistic listening
conditions (brisvox-bench denoise realistic ...). A second voice in a caller's line is usually a television or a
person in the next room: it comes out of a loudspeaker and through a room, not straight from a close microphone.

All audio here is 16 kHz, mono, 16-bit PCM. Nothing is committed mixed: the benchmark mixes the clips into the
caller fixtures at fixed levels and, for the "room" interferers, convolves the dry clips with the impulse responses
below at run time. No endorsement by the corpus authors, speakers or publishers is implied.

1. Dry clips (clips.json, kind "dry"): ten more speakers (4 female, 6 male) of the test-clean set of LibriSpeech:
   Vassil Panayotov, Guoguo Chen, Daniel Povey, Sanjeev Khudanpur, "Librispeech: an ASR corpus based on public
   domain audio books", ICASSP 2015, https://www.openslr.org/12/ . CC BY 4.0,
   https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ ; the underlying LibriVox recordings are in the public domain.
   Modifications: one utterance of 3.5 to 5.5 s per speaker, decoded from FLAC, converted to 16 kHz mono PCM16 and
   peak-normalized to -6 dBFS. clips.json has the transcript, the speaker's sex (LibriSpeech SPEAKERS.TXT) and the
   SHA-256 of the original FLAC.

2. Far-field loudspeaker recordings (clips.json, kind "farfield"): six excerpts of the VOiCES corpus (Voices Obscured
   in Complex Environmental Settings): Colleen Richey et al., Interspeech 2018, https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.05053 ,
   https://iqtlabs.github.io/voices/ , https://registry.opendata.aws/lab41-sri-voices/ . CC BY 4.0. They are LibriSpeech
   utterances played through a loudspeaker in a real room (room 1, no distractor noise) and recorded by a microphone
   at the far end of the room (mic 05, "far"). Modifications: 5 s from 0.3 s before the speech starts, peak-normalized
   to -6 dBFS; the file name and the SHA-256 of the original WAV are in clips.json. The corpus has no transcripts of
   the excerpts here: the benchmark only needs how many words there are (clips.json "words", an estimate of 2.5 words
   a second, not an alignment).

3. Turkish broadcast speech (clips.json, kind "turkish"): three excerpts of MediaSpeech (Turkish part), Rostislav
   Kolobov et al., "MediaSpeech: Multilanguage ASR Benchmark and Dataset", arXiv:2103.16193, 2021,
   https://www.openslr.org/108/ . CC BY 4.0. Speech of TV and online news programmes. Modifications: seconds 1.0 to 6.0
   of a clip of about 14.5 s, decoded from FLAC, converted to 16 kHz mono PCM16, peak-normalized to -6 dBFS. The text
   in clips.json is the start of the clip's transcript in proportion to the excerpt (not an alignment), with the non-ASCII
   characters left out: the benchmark only counts its words. The speakers'
   sex is not known.

4. Room impulse responses (rirs/): eight measured impulse responses of the "Room Impulse Response and Noise Database"
   (OpenSLR 28), Tom Ko, Vijayaditya Peddinti, Daniel Povey, Michael L. Seltzer, Sanjeev Khudanpur, "A study on data
   augmentation of reverberant speech for robust speech recognition", ICASSP 2017, https://www.openslr.org/28/ .
   Apache License 2.0, https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0 . The database collects, among others, the REVERB
   challenge 2014 (RVB2014), the Aachen impulse response database (AIR) and the RWCP sound scene database; each entry
   of rirs/rirs.json names the original file inside the OpenSLR archive and the room's reverberation time (the 60 dB
   decay time estimated from the -5 to -25 dB range of the energy decay curve of the whole response). Modifications:
   the first channel, resampled to 16 kHz where needed, starting 2 ms before the direct sound and cut to at most
   0.6 s with a 100 ms fade-out, peak-normalized to 0.9 (reverberation of the large rooms is therefore cut short).

5. speakers' sex: LibriSpeech SPEAKERS.TXT (same license as the corpus); `../callers/sex.json` holds the callers'.
